const e=[{id:"sv-3-431",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is a **multi-vari study**, as defined in the book?",options:["A designed experiment that changes several factors at once to measure their effects","A tool that graphically displays patterns of variation to find families of X's","A chart that ranks defect categories from the most to the least frequent","A table that scores the risk of each failure mode with an RPN"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the book's one-line definition: what does the chart display?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book defines multi-vari as a tool that **graphically displays patterns of variation**, to find possible X's or families of variation within a subgroup, between subgroups or over time."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"DOE changes factors on purpose, Pareto ranks categories and FMEA scores risk — none of them separates families of variation."}],final:"Graphical display of patterns of variation → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that describes a designed experiment (DOE); a multi-vari study in Analyze is passive.","","Incorrect — that describes a Pareto chart, which prioritises categories but does not separate families of variation.","Incorrect — that describes an FMEA, a Measure-phase risk tool."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-432",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In which DMAIC activity is a multi-vari study MAINLY used?",options:["Monitoring the improved process with control limits","Validating the measurement system before baselining","Writing the problem statement in the charter","X-sifting, early in the Analyze phase"],answer:3,hint:"Think of the funnel that takes the team from many possible X's to the vital few.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book opens Analyze with **X-sifting**: narrowing the probable X's to the vital few. Multi-vari is its first tool."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Control charts, MSA and the charter belong to Control, Measure and Define."}],final:"Multi-vari = first X-sifting tool of Analyze → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — monitoring belongs to Control (SPC charts and the control plan).","Incorrect — that is MSA (Gage R&R) in Measure.","Incorrect — the charter is a Define deliverable; no process data are analysed yet.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 240–241"},{id:"sv-3-433",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which tool isolates **within-unit, between-unit and over-time** variation on a single graph?",options:["Multi-vari chart","Pareto chart","Failure Mode and Effects Analysis","Shewhart control chart"],answer:0,hint:"Which tool is built around the three classic families of variation?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The multi-vari chart is designed to show **within-unit (positional), between-unit (cyclical) and temporal** variation together."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Pareto prioritises, FMEA scores risk and a Shewhart chart detects special causes over time only."}],final:"Three families on one graph = multi-vari → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency; it does not split variation into families.","Incorrect — FMEA ranks failure modes by risk; it uses no process data to separate variation.","Incorrect — a Shewhart chart separates common from special causes over time, not the three families."],bookRef:"Book pp. 241–243"},{id:"sv-3-434",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which describes **cyclical** variation in a multi-vari study?",options:["Differences between positions inside one unit made at the same moment","Differences between consecutive units, groups or batches","Differences between shifts, days or weeks","Differences caused by the measurement gauge itself"],answer:1,hint:"Cyclical is the 'between-unit' family.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Cyclical = **between-unit**: consecutive pieces, groups of pieces or consecutive batches."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Inside one unit is positional; shift/day/week is temporal; gauge error is MSA."}],final:"Cyclical = consecutive units or batches → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is positional (within-unit) variation.","","Incorrect — that is temporal variation, even though shifts repeat.","Incorrect — gauge variation is studied with MSA; it is not one of the three families."],trap:"Shifts repeat, but shift-to-shift is temporal, not cyclical.",bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-435",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's injection-moulding example, each die cycle produces 4 widgets. What is one **unit** in that multi-vari study?",options:["One widget measured at one position","All the die cycles made in one hour","All the widgets made in one day","The 4 widgets made in one die cycle"],answer:3,hint:"A unit can be one piece or a group of pieces made at the same moment.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: a unit can be a group of pieces made at one unique time. One die cycle makes 4 widgets at once, so **unit = the 4 widgets of one cycle**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Within-unit compares the 4 widgets; between-unit compares consecutive cycles; temporal compares hours."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An hour or a day is the temporal level, not the unit."}],final:"Unit = the 4 widgets of one die cycle → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — positions on a single widget would be finer than the unit the book defines.","Incorrect — one hour is a time period, the temporal level of the plan.","Incorrect — a day is a temporal period, not a unit.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 243–244"},{id:"sv-3-436",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why does the book check that a multi-vari data set shows at least **80% of the usual process variation**?",options:["To make sure the study captured the conditions that create the problem","To prove that the dominant family is statistically significant","To show that the measurement system is adequate","To confirm that the data are normally distributed"],answer:0,hint:"What goes wrong if the study samples only a quiet period?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Step 4 of the method asks whether the variation is exposed. A study that misses the usual spread may show an irrelevant family."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only when the captured spread is about 80% or more of the usual spread can the chart be trusted to represent the problem."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Significance, MSA and normality each have their own tools."}],final:"80% guideline = enough of the real variation was captured → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statistical significance needs a hypothesis test, not a coverage check.","Incorrect — measurement adequacy is judged with Gage R&R (%Study Var), not this guideline.","Incorrect — normality is checked with a normality test such as Anderson-Darling."],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-437",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the MAIN limitation of a multi-vari study?",options:["It needs the factors to be changed deliberately during the study","It shows where variation lives but does not prove the cause","It cannot show more than one family of variation at a time","It can only be used on manufacturing processes"],answer:1,hint:"Think about what a picture of passive data can and cannot establish.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A multi-vari chart points to a **family** of variation. The cause must still be confirmed with a hypothesis test and process knowledge."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"It is passive, shows all three families together and works in transactional settings too."}],final:"Points to a family, never proves cause → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the opposite: in Analyze the study is passive and changes nothing.","","Incorrect — showing all three families on one graph is its main strength.","Incorrect — the book also applies it to a call-centre process, though the data are noisier."],trap:"'The chart proved the root cause' is always a wrong conclusion.",bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-438",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How does the **X-Y Matrix** support a multi-vari study?",options:["It calculates the size of each family of variation","It confirms the dominant family with a p-value","It defines the balanced sampling plan","It provides the list of potential X's that are assigned to families"],answer:3,hint:"Where do the candidate X's come from before Analyze?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Before graphing, the book lists the potential X's from the Measure-phase **X-Y Matrix** and assigns each one to a family (splitting with percentages if needed)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The X-Y Matrix is organised brainstorming; it does not size families, test them or plan sampling."}],final:"X-Y Matrix supplies the X's to assign to families → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — family sizes are read from the multi-vari chart itself.","Incorrect — confirmation comes from a hypothesis test such as ANOVA.","Incorrect — the sampling plan is built in step 1 of the multi-vari method, not in the X-Y Matrix.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 245"},{id:"sv-3-439",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How is a multi-vari chart used to narrow the list of potential X's?",options:["By focusing on the X's of the largest family and parking the others","By ranking the X's on their RPN scores","By removing every X whose correlation with Y is below 0.8","By plotting the X's against the specification limits"],answer:0,hint:"The study tells you what the problem is AND what it is not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Once the largest family is known, the X's that act through it rise to the top; X's tied to small families are parked."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"RPN, correlation thresholds and specification limits belong to FMEA, correlation and capability."}],final:"Keep the X's of the dominant family → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — RPN ranking is the FMEA's method.","Incorrect — a multi-vari chart gives no correlation coefficients; that idea belongs to correlation analysis.","Incorrect — specification limits belong to capability analysis, not X-sifting."],bookRef:"Book p. 245"},{id:"sv-3-440",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A multi-vari chart suggests that the **three time periods** differ most. Which test is the natural choice to confirm this family with continuous data?",options:["Chi-square test of independence","One-way ANOVA","Two-proportion test","Paired t-test"],answer:1,hint:"Continuous Y, one categorical factor with three levels.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book says to confirm the suspected family with the appropriate hypothesis test."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Comparing the means of a continuous Y across **three** periods = one-way ANOVA."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Chi-square and proportions need attribute data; paired t handles two related samples."}],final:"Three period means, continuous Y → one-way ANOVA → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — chi-square compares two categorical variables, not means of a continuous Y.","","Incorrect — a proportion test needs attribute (pass/fail) data.","Incorrect — a paired t-test compares two related measurements, not three independent periods."],bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-441",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does it mean that a multi-vari study is **passive** at this point in DMAIC?",options:["The data are taken only from historical records, never collected new","The Black Belt does not interpret the chart until the Control phase","Only one source of variation is studied at a time","The process is observed as it normally runs, without changing any factor"],answer:3,hint:"Passive is the opposite of experimenting.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: at this point it is a **passive** study — you watch the process and do not change factors. Later the same chart can display experimental data."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Passive does not mean 'historical only', 'later' or 'one factor'."}],final:"Passive = observe, change nothing → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — new data are usually gathered to a sampling plan; passive means no factor is changed.","Incorrect — the chart is interpreted straight away in Analyze.","Incorrect — the study looks at several sources together.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-442",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In the book's **bottle-fill** exercise, the within-unit family was the largest. What was the cause?",options:["Air flow in the room disturbing the in-line scale","Worn filling nozzles on two of the twelve heads","A different operator setting up each shift","Changes in bottle supplier from batch to batch"],answer:0,hint:"The surprise in this exercise involved how the fill was measured.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: positive room pressure blew air across the **in-line scale**, so recorded weights fluctuated across bottles filled together."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A large within-unit family can point at the measurement system itself."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Shift and batch effects would show as temporal and between-unit variation."}],final:"In-line scale disturbed by air flow → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book traced the pattern to the measurement, not to nozzle wear.","Incorrect — operator-to-shift effects would appear as temporal variation.","Incorrect — batch-to-batch effects would show as between-unit variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 253"},{id:"sv-3-443",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which improvement principle does the book draw from the bottle-fill exercise, where the mean fill was well above the upper limit?",options:["Shift the mean first, then reduce the variation","Reduce variation first, then shift the mean","Stabilise the process before measuring the baseline","Transform the data before reading the chart"],answer:1,hint:"With a wide spread, moving the mean risks pushing product outside the other limit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"After fixing the scale problem, the variation shrank and the mean could then be moved toward the specification."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The book's lesson: **reduce variation first, then shift the mean**."}],final:"Variation first, then the mean → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book's order is the reverse.","","Incorrect — the book warns against stabilising before baselining; this is not the bottle-fill lesson.","Incorrect — the multi-vari chart is read on the raw continuous data."],bookRef:"Book p. 253"},{id:"sv-3-444",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Why is a multi-vari chart of **call-centre** handle time usually harder to read than one from a moulding process?",options:["Call times are attribute data, which the chart cannot display","Transactional data can never be balanced","Incoming calls are not controlled, so one long call can mislead a mean","Minitab cannot draw multi-vari charts for service data"],answer:2,hint:"What is different about the 'parts' entering a contact centre?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In the book's call-centre example, calls vary widely and are not controlled, so an outlier can sway a mean and the largest family is less obvious."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The advice: show the individual points and use hypothesis tests rather than force-fit a graphical verdict."}],final:"Uncontrolled, noisy transactions → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — call time is continuous; the difficulty is noise, not data type.","Incorrect — call data can be sampled in a balanced plan.","","Incorrect — the book draws a call-centre multi-vari chart in Minitab."],bookRef:"Book pp. 248–250"},{id:"sv-3-445",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which order of factors does the book call the **classical** order for a multi-vari chart?",options:["Temporal, between-unit, within-unit","Between-unit, within-unit, temporal","Within-unit, temporal, between-unit","Within-unit, between-unit, temporal"],answer:3,hint:"Think inside-out: positions sit inside units, units inside periods.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The classical order is **within, between, temporal** — from the innermost level of the nested plan to the outermost."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The book adds that any order may be used if it shows the variation more clearly."}],final:"Classical order = within, between, temporal → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is the classical order reversed.","Incorrect — the classical order starts with the innermost family.","Incorrect — temporal is the outermost family and comes last.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 253"},{id:"sv-3-446",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is **X-sifting** in the Analyze phase?",options:["Using process data to separate the vital few X's from the trivial many","Brainstorming every possible X on a fishbone diagram","Controlling the critical X's with SPC charts","Removing X's from the process with poka-yoke devices"],answer:0,hint:"Sifting means narrowing down.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's X funnel: possible X's → probable X's → **X-sifting** with data → the vital few to prove and fix."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Fishbone generates X's; SPC and poka-yoke control or remove them later."}],final:"X-sifting = data narrows X's to the vital few → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — brainstorming possible X's happens in Measure with the fishbone.","Incorrect — controlling X's is a Control-phase activity.","Incorrect — mistake-proofing is a Control/Improve solution, not a sifting step."],bookRef:"Book p. 240"},{id:"sv-3-447",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"In Minitab, a multi-vari chart is built from one response and **up to how many** factors?",options:["Two","Three","Four","Six"],answer:2,hint:"Recall the Minitab multi-vari dialog: response plus factors 1 to …",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Minitab's multi-vari chart takes one continuous response and **up to four** categorical factors."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A classic three-family study uses three of them."}],final:"Up to four factors → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Minitab allows more than two factors on a multi-vari chart.","Incorrect — three is a common choice (within, between, temporal) but not the limit.","","Incorrect — Minitab's multi-vari dialog accepts at most four factors."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.1"},{id:"sv-3-448",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which structure must a multi-vari sampling plan have?",options:["Random, with no grouping, so that families cannot bias the result","Nested: positions within units, and units within time periods","Stratified by customer segment only","Paired: every unit measured before and after a change"],answer:1,hint:"Each family must sit inside the next one.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The plan must cover **within, between and temporal** variation, so it is nested: several positions in each unit, several units in each period, several periods."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Random, stratified or paired designs serve other tools."}],final:"Nested plan: positions → units → periods → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — random ungrouped data cannot separate the three families.","","Incorrect — stratifying by customer is not the multi-vari structure.","Incorrect — before/after pairing belongs to a paired t-test design."],bookRef:"Book pp. 242–244"},{id:"sv-3-449",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"According to the book, which analysis does a multi-vari chart illustrate in graphical form?",options:["Analysis of variance","Capability analysis","Measurement system analysis","Regression"],answer:0,hint:"The chart splits total variation by categorical source.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: the multi-vari chart **illustrates analysis-of-variance data in a graph** — variation broken down by categorical source."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is why ANOVA is the natural follow-up to confirm a family."}],final:"Graphical ANOVA → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a multi-vari chart can hint at capability, but it illustrates the variance breakdown by source.","Incorrect — MSA splits gauge variation, not process families.","Incorrect — regression models Y as a function of a continuous X."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-450",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"In multi-vari terms, what is a **family of variation**?",options:["A set of defect categories ranked on a Pareto chart","The variation between appraisers in a Gage R&R","A group of causes that produce the same pattern of variation","The difference between control limits and specification limits"],answer:2,hint:"Positional, cyclical and temporal are the three classic ones.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A family groups causes that create the same pattern: within-unit, between-unit or over time."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Pareto categories, reproducibility and limit comparisons belong to other tools."}],final:"Family = causes with the same variation pattern → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Pareto categories are defect types, not families of variation.","Incorrect — that is reproducibility, an MSA term.","","Incorrect — that compares voice of the process with voice of the customer."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-451",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What is the PRIMARY output of a multi-vari study?",options:["A p-value for each potential X","A prediction equation for Y","A risk priority number for each failure mode","A picture showing which family of variation is largest"],answer:3,hint:"The study is graphical.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The multi-vari study produces a **chart** showing the size of each family; the largest one directs the next step."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"P-values, equations and RPNs come from tests, regression and FMEA."}],final:"A picture of the dominant family → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p-values come from the hypothesis tests that follow the chart.","Incorrect — prediction equations come from regression.","Incorrect — RPNs come from an FMEA.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-452",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What is the main advantage of a multi-vari study over testing potential X's one at a time?",options:["It screens many X's at once, cheaply, by grouping them into families","It proves cause and effect without a hypothesis test","It removes the need for a balanced sampling plan","It works equally well with pass/fail outputs"],answer:0,hint:"Why is it the FIRST sieve rather than the last?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"One passive, nested study shows all three families together; whole groups of X's become plausible or implausible at a glance."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"It does not prove cause, needs balanced data and needs a continuous Y."}],final:"Screens many X's at once → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — it never proves cause; tests and process knowledge are still needed.","Incorrect — the chart actually requires balanced data.","Incorrect — the output must be continuous."],bookRef:"Book pp. 240–245"},{id:"sv-3-453",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A Black Belt wants to run a multi-vari study. Which output (Y) is suitable?",options:["Whether each order was shipped on time (yes/no)","The time in minutes to provision each order","The defect category recorded on each return","The customer segment that placed each order"],answer:1,hint:"The output must be measured on a continuous scale.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multi-vari needs a **continuous** Y and **discrete** sources of variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Provisioning time in minutes is continuous; the others are attribute or categorical."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Segment could serve as a source of variation (X), never as the Y."}],final:"Continuous Y = provisioning minutes → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a yes/no outcome is attribute data; multi-vari needs a continuous Y.","","Incorrect — a category is nominal data, not a continuous output.","Incorrect — a segment is a categorical source of variation, not a Y."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-454",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the three families of variation is CORRECT?",options:["Day-to-day differences are an example of cyclical variation","Positional variation compares consecutive batches","Chips across one silicon wafer show within-unit variation","Temporal variation is another name for within-piece variation"],answer:2,hint:"Match each family to its book examples.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book examples: within-unit = one part's geometry, **many chips on one wafer**; between-unit = consecutive pieces or batches; temporal = shift, day, week."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Each wrong statement swaps two families."}],final:"Chips on one wafer = within-unit → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — day-to-day differences are temporal variation.","Incorrect — consecutive batches are between-unit (cyclical) variation.","","Incorrect — within-piece is positional; temporal is time-to-time."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-455",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about **balanced** data for a multi-vari chart is CORRECT?",options:["The data must be normally distributed in every cell","Balance means the Y and X's have the same units","Balance is optional because Minitab fills missing cells with the mean","Every source of variation must have the same number of data points"],answer:3,hint:"Think about what Minitab checks before drawing the chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: in Minitab the data must be **balanced** — every source of variation has the same number of data points."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Normality, units and automatic filling are not what balance means."}],final:"Equal data points per source → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — balance is about equal counts, not normality.","Incorrect — Y is continuous and X's are categories; units are irrelevant to balance.","Incorrect — Minitab will not draw the chart from unbalanced data.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 241, 254"},{id:"sv-3-456",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about the book's **80% guideline** for multi-vari studies is CORRECT?",options:["The spread in the study should be at least 80% of the usual process spread","At least 80% of the readings must fall inside the specification limits","The dominant family must explain at least 80% of the variation","At least 80% of potential X's must be included in the plan"],answer:0,hint:"It compares two spreads.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: the data should show **at least 80% of the usual process variation** (moulding example 0.22 of 0.25 = 88%)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Specification coverage, one family's share and the number of X's are different ideas."}],final:"Study spread ≥ 80% of usual spread → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the guideline compares spreads; it has nothing to do with specification limits.","Incorrect — the guideline is about how much usual variation the sample captured, not one family's share.","Incorrect — the guideline concerns variation captured, not the number of X's."],bookRef:"Book pp. 242, 247"},{id:"sv-3-457",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about multi-vari studies and experiments is CORRECT?",options:["A multi-vari chart can only display passive data","In Analyze the study is passive, but the chart can later show data from an experiment","A multi-vari study must always be run as a full factorial experiment","Experiments are run first, and multi-vari charts confirm them"],answer:1,hint:"What can change between Analyze and later work?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: at this point it is a passive study; **later** the same chart can display data from experimentation where factors are changed on purpose."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other statements reverse or over-restrict this."}],final:"Passive now, experimental data possible later → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book says the same chart can later show experimental data.","","Incorrect — in Analyze no factors are deliberately changed.","Incorrect — the order is the reverse: multi-vari screens first, experiments come later."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-458",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about reading a multi-vari chart is CORRECT?",options:["The family with the most consistent pattern is always the dominant one","The dominant family is the one entered first in Minitab","The family with the largest spread is the dominant one","All three families must be equal before the chart is valid"],answer:2,hint:"Compare the sizes, not the regularity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Size each family: line length within units, jumps of unit means, gaps between period means. **The largest spread wins.**"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A position that is always slightly high is consistent but may be small; factor order only changes the picture."}],final:"Largest spread = dominant family → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — consistency is not size; a small but regular pattern can sit beside a much larger family.","Incorrect — factor order changes the look of the chart, not which family is largest.","","Incorrect — unequal families are exactly what the chart is meant to reveal."],trap:"A consistent pattern is not the same as a large one.",bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-459",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the result of the book's **injection-moulding** multi-vari study is CORRECT?",options:["Within-unit variation was largest, so cavity geometry was investigated","Temporal variation was largest, so hour-to-hour X's were investigated","All families were equal, so a designed experiment was run immediately","Between-unit variation was largest, so X's acting on each die cycle were investigated"],answer:3,hint:"Recall the three spreads the book read off the chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book readings: within ≈ 0.07, **between ≈ 0.18**, temporal ≈ 0.06."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"'Something is changing from die cycle to die cycle', so X's such as injection pressure and die temperature were the focus."}],final:"Between-unit (die cycle to die cycle) dominated → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — within-unit spread was about 0.07, smaller than between-unit.","Incorrect — temporal spread was about 0.06, the smallest family.","Incorrect — the families clearly differed; between-unit dominated.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-460",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about multi-vari analysis of **transactional** processes is TRUE?",options:["Showing the individual data points helps spot unusually long or short transactions","The largest family is usually easier to see than in manufacturing","Transactional Ys must be converted to pass/fail before charting","Hypothesis tests should be avoided because the chart is enough"],answer:0,hint:"What Minitab option did the book use on the call-centre chart?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: in transactional data an outlier can mislead you; **display the individual points** to see very long or short calls."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The book also says not to force-fit the tool and to use hypothesis tests where needed."}],final:"Show individual points in noisy transactional data → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book says it is harder to see in the call-centre example.","Incorrect — the Y stays continuous (e.g. call time).","Incorrect — the book advises hypothesis tests rather than force-fitting the chart."],bookRef:"Book pp. 248–250"},{id:"sv-3-461",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about what follows a multi-vari chart is CORRECT?",options:["The chart's result should go straight into the control plan","The dominant family should be confirmed with an appropriate hypothesis test","The dominant family should be fixed by re-centring the process mean","The study must be repeated until all families are equal"],answer:1,hint:"Graphical first, statistical second.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book next steps: confirm the family with the right **hypothesis test**, focus on its X's, then collect more data or experiment."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Control plans, re-centring and repeating the study are not the next step."}],final:"Confirm statistically, then dig into the family's X's → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — control plans come after solutions are proven in Improve.","","Incorrect — a family must be understood before any adjustment; this jumps to a solution.","Incorrect — equal families are not a goal; the aim is to find the largest one."],bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-462",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the data needed for a multi-vari study are CORRECT?

1. The output (Y) must be continuous.
2. The sources of variation must be discrete categories.
3. Factors must be changed deliberately during the study.
4. Unbalanced data can be charted as long as every cell has at least one reading.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Check each statement against the book's data rules: Y type, X type, passive, balanced.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book rules: **continuous Y**, **discrete sources**, a **passive** study and **balanced** data."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 1 and 2 match; 3 breaks 'passive' and 4 breaks 'balanced'."}],final:"True pair = 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — statement 3 is false: in Analyze the study is passive and nothing is changed.","Incorrect — both are false: the study is passive and Minitab needs balanced data.","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: every source needs the same number of data points."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-463",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`A moulding team classifies four comparisons. Which statements are CORRECT?

1. Monday versus Friday output is cyclical variation.
2. The four cavities of one shot show within-unit variation.
3. Consecutive batches of resin show between-unit variation.
4. Hour-to-hour differences are positional variation.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Within = inside one unit; between = consecutive units; temporal = time periods.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Positional = within one unit at one time; cyclical = consecutive units or batches; temporal = hours, days, shifts."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cavities of one shot = within (2 true); consecutive batches = between (3 true); 1 and 4 are temporal."}],final:"True pair = 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: Monday versus Friday is temporal variation.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: hour-to-hour differences are temporal, not positional.","Incorrect — both are false: day and hour comparisons are temporal variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-464",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`Which statements describe genuine limitations of a passive multi-vari study?

1. It proves which X causes the dominant family.
2. It removes the need for any later hypothesis test.
3. X's that never changed during the study stay invisible.
4. X's that change together during the study cannot be separated.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"A limitation is something the tool cannot do; a false claim is not a limitation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A passive study only sees X's that moved, and X's that move together are confounded."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 3 and 4 are real limitations; 1 and 2 claim powers the tool does not have."}],final:"True pair = 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both are false claims; a multi-vari chart proves nothing and must be followed by tests.","Incorrect — statement 2 is a false claim, not a limitation: hypothesis tests still follow.","","Incorrect — statement 1 is a false claim: the chart does not prove cause."],bookRef:"Book pp. 245–247"},{id:"sv-3-465",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`A multi-vari chart has identified the dominant family. Which statements describe the book's recommended next steps?

1. Confirm the family with an appropriate hypothesis test.
2. Recalculate the control limits of the process.
3. Write the control plan for the dominant family.
4. Focus on the X's that belong to the dominant family.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Stay in Analyze: confirm, then focus.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: confirm the family statistically, focus on its X's, then gather more data or experiment."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 1 and 4 are Analyze steps; 2 and 3 belong to Control."}],final:"True pair = 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 2 is wrong: control limits belong to Control, not to X-sifting.","Incorrect — both are Control-phase activities that come much later.","Incorrect — statement 3 is wrong: a control plan comes after solutions are proven.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-466",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

At this point in DMAIC a multi-vari study is a [?] study: the process is observed without changing any factor.`,options:["active","designed","passive","randomised"],answer:2,hint:"The study only watches the process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book calls the Analyze multi-vari study **passive**: data are gathered as the process runs."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Active, designed and randomised all describe experiments."}],final:"Passive study → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an active study would involve intervening in the process.","Incorrect — a designed experiment changes factors on purpose.","","Incorrect — randomisation is a feature of experiments, not of observing the process."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-467",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Variation from one die cycle to the next die cycle is called [?] variation.`,options:["cyclical","positional","systematic","temporal"],answer:0,hint:"Consecutive units belong to the between-unit family.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Between consecutive units (die cycles) = **cyclical** (between-unit) variation."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Positional is within the cycle; temporal is hour to hour."}],final:"Cyclical variation → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — positional variation is within one unit, e.g. the cavities of one die cycle.","Incorrect — 'systematic' is not one of the three multi-vari families.","Incorrect — temporal variation compares longer periods such as hours or shifts."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-468",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Before interpreting a multi-vari chart, the book's guideline is that the data should show at least [?] of the usual process variation.`,options:["50%","70%","80%","95%"],answer:2,hint:"Recall the moulding example: 88% was judged enough.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book guideline: at least **80%** of the usual variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"In the moulding case 0.22 of 0.25 = 88%, above the guideline."}],final:"80% → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — half the usual spread is too little; the study may have missed the problem.","Incorrect — 70% is the pass mark of the exam, not the book's coverage guideline.","","Incorrect — 95% is a common confidence level, not this guideline."],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-469",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

In Minitab, multi-vari data must be [?], which means every source of variation has the same number of data points.`,options:["balanced","normal","randomised","stacked"],answer:0,hint:"Equal counts in every condition.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book: data must be **balanced** — label units generically so every combination has equal counts."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Normality, randomisation and stacking are other ideas."}],final:"Balanced → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — normality is not required to draw a multi-vari chart.","Incorrect — randomised run order belongs to experiments.","Incorrect — stacking is a worksheet layout, not the equal-count requirement."],bookRef:"Book pp. 241, 254"},{id:"sv-3-470",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.

A multi-vari study is the first [?] tool of the Analyze phase.`,options:["control","measurement","solution","X-sifting"],answer:3,hint:"Analyze narrows the probable X's.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: Analyze begins with **X-sifting**, and multi-vari is its first tool."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Control, measurement and solution tools belong to other phases."}],final:"X-sifting → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — control tools such as SPC belong to the Control phase.","Incorrect — measurement tools such as Gage R&R belong to Measure.","Incorrect — solutions are generated and tested in Improve.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 240"},{id:"sv-3-471",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT one of the families (or family names) used in a multi-vari study?",options:["Cyclical","Positional","Reproducibility","Temporal"],answer:2,hint:"One option belongs to Gage R&R.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The three classic families are positional, cyclical and temporal."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"**Reproducibility** is appraiser-to-appraiser variation in a Gage R&R study."}],final:"Reproducibility is an MSA term → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — cyclical (between-unit) is a multi-vari family.","Incorrect — positional (within-unit) is a multi-vari family.","","Incorrect — temporal (over time) is a multi-vari family."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-472",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT an example of **temporal** variation?",options:["Differences between the morning and night shifts","Differences between week 1 and week 3","Differences between the cavities of one moulding shot","Differences between the 08:00 and 10:00 hours"],answer:2,hint:"Temporal compares time periods.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Temporal = shift, hour, day or week comparisons."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cavities of one shot are made at the same moment — **within-unit (positional)** variation."}],final:"Cavities of one shot = positional → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — shift-to-shift is temporal.","Incorrect — week-to-week is temporal.","","Incorrect — hour-to-hour comparisons of averages are temporal."],bookRef:"Book pp. 243–244"},{id:"sv-3-473",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT one of the five steps of the book's multi-vari method?",options:["Create a sampling plan","Check whether the variation is exposed","Gather passive data","Calculate the risk priority number"],answer:3,hint:"Plan, gather, graph, check, interpret.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book method: create sampling plan → gather passive data → graph → check variation exposed → interpret."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The **RPN** belongs to an FMEA."}],final:"RPN is an FMEA step → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this is step 1.","Incorrect — this is step 4.","Incorrect — this is step 2.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-474",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Besides the families of variation, a multi-vari chart can display several other things. All of the following can be seen on it EXCEPT:",options:["The risk priority number of each failure mode","Two-way interactions between factors","The stability of the output over the study","The effect of shifts, machines or materials on Y"],answer:0,hint:"One option comes from a Measure-phase risk tool.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: the chart can also show capability, stability, two-way interactions and the effect of shifts, machines, materials or noise."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"**RPN** values come only from an FMEA."}],final:"RPN cannot be read from a multi-vari chart → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book lists interactions among what the chart can show.","Incorrect — the book says the chart can show stability.","Incorrect — the book lists these effects explicitly."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-475",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"A multi-vari study of **cable diameter** shows that the temporal (day-to-day) family is dominant. Which X is LEAST likely to deserve attention first?",options:["Ambient temperature changing from day to day","The position of the sensor across the cable's width","A worn die that is replaced every week","Different raw-material lots used on different days"],answer:1,hint:"Which X acts within one unit rather than over time?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Keep the X's that act through the dominant family; park the others."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Sensor position across the width creates **positional** variation, which is small here."}],final:"Positional X is parked when temporal dominates → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — ambient temperature varies over days and fits a temporal family.","","Incorrect — tool wear and replacement act over time, a classic temporal X.","Incorrect — a daily lot change shows up as day-to-day variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 245"},{id:"sv-3-476",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"All four data sets below are balanced. Which one is NOT suitable for drawing a multi-vari chart?",options:["Fill volume (ml) by filling head, cycle and hour, 3 readings per cell","Call time (s) by agent and hour, 5 calls per cell","Pass/fail result of each call audit by agent and hour, 5 audits per cell","Splice loss (dB) by tray, cabinet, week and technician, 2 readings per cell"],answer:2,hint:"Balance is satisfied everywhere; check the type of Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multi-vari needs a **continuous** Y and up to four categorical factors."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A pass/fail audit result is attribute data, so it cannot be the Y of a multi-vari chart."}],final:"Attribute Y cannot be charted → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — continuous Y with three categorical factors is a classic multi-vari data set.","Incorrect — continuous Y with two categorical factors can be charted.","","Incorrect — four factors is within Minitab's limit and the Y is continuous."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-477",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A Black Belt has a continuous Y (order-fulfilment time) and a long list of categorical suspects: warehouse zone, picking wave and day. Before running formal tests she wants one picture showing whether most variation sits within a wave, between waves or between days. Which tool should she use?",options:["Fishbone diagram","Scatter plot","Regression analysis","Multi-vari chart"],answer:3,hint:"One picture, continuous Y, nested categorical sources.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A multi-vari chart shows within, between and over-time variation for a continuous Y and categorical sources."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Within a wave, between waves, between days = the three families."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Fishbone brainstorms; scatter and regression need continuous X's."}],final:"Nested categorical sources, one picture → multi-vari → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a fishbone lists possible causes; it uses no data to size variation.","Incorrect — a scatter plot relates two continuous variables, not nested categorical sources.","Incorrect — regression models Y from continuous X's and comes later.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-478",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"In an electroplating line, 12 parts hang on one rack at top, middle and bottom positions of the tank, and the whole rack is plated at once. Coating thickness differs mainly between the top and bottom parts of the **same** rack. Which family of variation is this?",options:["Within-unit (positional)","Between-unit (cyclical)","Temporal","Common-cause"],answer:0,hint:"The 12 parts of one rack form one unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book's within-unit examples include **position in a plating batch**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Top versus bottom on the same rack, plated at the same moment = positional variation."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Rack-to-rack is cyclical; hour-to-hour is temporal."}],final:"Position in one plating batch = within-unit → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — between-unit would compare one rack with the next rack.","Incorrect — temporal would compare hours, shifts or days.","Incorrect — common cause is an SPC term, not one of the three families."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-479",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"An accounts-payable team processes invoices in back-to-back batches of 50. Average processing time jumps noticeably from one batch to the next batch, while invoices inside a batch are similar and the daily averages are steady. Which family dominates?",options:["Positional","Cyclical","Temporal","Measurement"],answer:1,hint:"Consecutive batches = between-unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Consecutive batches made close together in time are between-unit (**cyclical**) variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Batch-to-batch jumps are large; within-batch and day-to-day are small."}],final:"Batch-to-batch = cyclical → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — positional variation would be large inside a batch, which is not the case.","","Incorrect — the daily averages are steady, so temporal variation is small.","Incorrect — measurement variation is studied with MSA, not a multi-vari family."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-480",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A multi-vari study of **cable-extrusion diameter** shows little variation along one reel and little from week to week, but a large jump from one reel to the next reel. Which X's should the team investigate first?",options:["Sensor position across the cable width","Seasonal changes in ambient humidity","Reel changeover set-up and the material lot loaded for each reel","The number of shifts worked per week"],answer:2,hint:"Which X's change every time a new unit starts?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Reel-to-reel = between-unit (cyclical) variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"X's that change at each reel — **changeover set-up and material lot** — fit that family."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Sensor position is positional; humidity and shifts are temporal."}],final:"Reel-to-reel jumps → changeover and lot X's → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that would create within-unit variation, which is small.","Incorrect — seasonal effects are temporal, which is small here.","","Incorrect — shift patterns act over time, a temporal X."],bookRef:"Book pp. 245–247"},{id:"sv-3-481",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"An 8-head capping machine caps 8 bottles at once. A multi-vari study of cap torque shows that the spread among the 8 bottles of **one** cycle is far larger than cycle-to-cycle or hour-to-hour differences. Which X's should be examined first?",options:["Material lot changes between hours","Operator differences between shifts","The warm-up period after start-up","Head-specific X's such as each head's clutch setting, and the torque measurement"],answer:3,hint:"The 8 bottles of one cycle are one unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Spread inside one cycle = within-unit (positional) variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Positional X's are those that differ by head — clutch settings, alignment — and, as in the book's bottle-fill case, the measuring device itself."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Lots, shifts and warm-up act over time."}],final:"Within-cycle spread → head-specific X's (and the gauge) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — lot changes would appear as temporal variation, which is small.","Incorrect — shift effects are temporal, which is small here.","Incorrect — warm-up acts over time, a temporal X.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 245, 253"},{id:"sv-3-482",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A quality team wants to study **whether each customer call passed the compliance audit (yes/no)** across agents, hours and days. A colleague suggests a multi-vari chart. What is the MOST appropriate response?",options:["Use a different tool, because the output is attribute data, not continuous","Agree, because multi-vari works with any type of output","Agree, but only if the data are first made normal with a transformation","Use multi-vari, but enter the agents as the output"],answer:0,hint:"Check the data type of Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multi-vari needs a **continuous** Y with discrete sources of variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Pass/fail is attribute data; proportion tests or chi-square fit better."}],final:"Attribute Y → not a multi-vari problem → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — multi-vari requires a continuous output.","Incorrect — a yes/no result cannot be transformed into a continuous measurement.","Incorrect — agents are a categorical source of variation, not a Y."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-483",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A data-centre team measures **rack inlet temperature** at the top slot of the same rack at 02:00, 10:00 and 18:00 every day for two weeks. What do the 02:00 versus 18:00 comparisons represent?",options:["Positional variation","Temporal variation","Cyclical variation","Measurement variation"],answer:1,hint:"Same position, different times of day.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Temporal variation compares time periods: hours, shifts, days."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The same slot read at different hours = **time-to-time** variation."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Slot-to-slot is positional; consecutive readings are cyclical."}],final:"Time-of-day comparison = temporal → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — positional would compare slots inside the rack at the same moment.","","Incorrect — cyclical would compare consecutive readings taken close together.","Incorrect — measurement variation is assessed in MSA, not as a multi-vari family."],bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-484",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A Black Belt has gathered passive multi-vari data and drawn the chart. At step 4 she sees only minor variation, far less than the process usually shows. What is the BEST next action?",options:["Interpret the chart anyway and pick the largest family","Switch to a designed experiment immediately","Go back and collect more data so the usual variation is exposed","Recalculate the control limits on the process chart"],answer:2,hint:"Step 4 is a gate before interpretation.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book step 4: check whether the variation is exposed; if only minor variation appears, **go back and collect more data**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Extend the plan (more periods, busier times) before interpreting."}],final:"Collect more data before interpreting → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — interpreting a chart that missed the usual variation may point to an irrelevant family.","Incorrect — the book's fix is more passive data, not abandoning the tool.","","Incorrect — control limits are a Control-phase topic, unrelated to this check."],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-485",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A multi-vari chart showed the shift-to-shift family to be dominant. The Black Belt declared 'shift practices' the root cause and moved straight to Improve. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because the dominant family on a multi-vari chart identifies the root cause","Yes, because temporal families do not need statistical confirmation","No, because the family should first be confirmed with a hypothesis test and its X's investigated","No, because shift-to-shift variation belongs to the cyclical family"],answer:2,hint:"What does the chart point to, and what must follow?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The chart points to a family; the book's next step is to confirm it with a test and focus on that family's X's."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Jumping to Improve skips both confirmation and X investigation."}],final:"No — confirm and investigate first → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a family is not a root cause; many X's act through shifts.","Incorrect — every family needs confirmation; temporal is no exception.","","Incorrect — shift-to-shift is temporal; the family label was right."],bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-486",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A pilot multi-vari study was run on a public holiday. Its spread was about half of the spread the process usually shows. The Black Belt interpreted the chart because one family looked clearly dominant. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because a clearly dominant family is enough to interpret the chart","Yes, because public-holiday data remove noise from the study","No, because the study must always run for at least four weeks","No, because the data show well below 80% of the usual variation"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the captured spread with the book's guideline.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The data should show at least **80%** of the usual process variation before interpretation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"About 50% is far below the guideline; extend the study to normal working days."}],final:"No — below the 80% guideline → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a clear pattern on unrepresentative data can be the wrong family.","Incorrect — removing the usual conditions also removes the variation the team needs to see.","Incorrect — the book sets no fixed duration; the test is how much variation is captured.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-487",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"During a multi-vari study in Analyze, an engineer raised the oven temperature on day 2 'to see what happens'. Was this an appropriate action?",options:["Yes, because changing factors makes the families easier to see","Yes, because a multi-vari study is a type of designed experiment","No, because the study should observe the process as it normally runs","No, because temperature can only be studied with a control chart"],answer:2,hint:"What does 'passive' require?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At this point the multi-vari study is **passive**: no factor is changed on purpose."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Raising the temperature mixes an intervention into the data and confuses the temporal family."}],final:"No — keep the study passive → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — deliberate changes add variation that the passive study is not designed to separate.","Incorrect — in Analyze the study is passive; experiments come later.","","Incorrect — temperature could be a factor in a later experiment; the issue is changing it during a passive study."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-488",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A multi-vari study of **fill weight** shows the within-unit family (bottles filled at the same moment) is largest. All weights come from one in-line scale. What should the team do FIRST?",options:["Adjust the filling heads to move the mean toward target","Replace the filling machine with a newer model","Run a capability study on the current data","Check the measurement system, including the scale and its surroundings"],answer:3,hint:"Recall what caused the within-unit family in the book's bottle-fill exercise.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In the book, the within-unit family was caused by air flow disturbing the **in-line scale**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A dominant within-unit family measured on one gauge points first to the measurement system."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Adjusting, replacing or capability studies all rely on data that may be wrong."}],final:"Check the measurement system first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — shifting the mean before reducing variation, and before trusting the data, repeats the book's warning.","Incorrect — an expensive solution before the cause is known.","Incorrect — capability on data from a doubtful scale would be unreliable.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 253"},{id:"sv-3-489",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A team wants a multi-vari study of **ticket resolution time**, but the reporting system stores only one daily average per site. What is the BEST action?",options:["Collect individual ticket times to a nested sampling plan","Use the daily averages, because averages remove noise","Use the daily averages and label the sites 1 to n","Replace the multi-vari study with a Pareto chart of sites"],answer:0,hint:"A daily average can only show the temporal family.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The plan must expose within, between and temporal variation, so data are needed at the lowest level."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Collect individual tickets (e.g. tickets within agent, agents within day) to a balanced, nested plan."}],final:"Collect individual data to a nested plan → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — daily averages hide within-unit and between-unit variation, so two families vanish.","Incorrect — labelling does not recover the missing lower levels.","Incorrect — a Pareto chart ranks categories; it cannot show the families."],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-490",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"In a multi-vari study the Black Belt collected 5 tickets for most agents but only 3 for two agents who were on leave. Minitab will not draw the chart. What is the BEST action?",options:["Switch the response to the number of tickets per agent","Collect data so every agent-hour cell has the same number of tickets","Delete the hour factor so fewer cells are needed","Enter the two agents' averages three times to fill the gaps"],answer:1,hint:"Minitab needs equal counts in every condition.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multi-vari data must be **balanced**: every source has the same number of data points."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Collect the missing readings (or drop to an equal number per cell) so every cell matches."}],final:"Balance the data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that changes the Y to a count and loses the continuous measure.","","Incorrect — dropping a factor loses a family of variation.","Incorrect — inventing data distorts the variation the chart must show."],bookRef:"Book pp. 241, 254"},{id:"sv-3-491",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A multi-vari chart shows that **position 3** on every unit is always slightly higher than the others (by about 0.2 mm), while the period means differ by about 3 mm. Which X's should the team focus on FIRST?",options:["Position-specific X's, because position 3 is high every time","No X's, because the chart shows the process is stable","Time-related X's, because the temporal family is largest","Unit-to-unit X's, because consecutive units always differ"],answer:2,hint:"Size beats consistency.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The dominant family is the one with the **largest spread**, not the most regular pattern."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Temporal (~3 mm) far exceeds the positional effect (~0.2 mm); note position 3, but dig into time-related X's."}],final:"Temporal family dominates → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a consistent pattern is not a large one; 0.2 mm is small beside 3 mm.","Incorrect — large differences between periods are not a sign of stability.","","Incorrect — the stem gives no evidence of large between-unit jumps."],trap:"A consistent pattern is not the same as a large one.",bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-492",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team read the three family spreads off a multi-vari chart of **seal strength (N)**:

\`\`\`
Family spread summary (read from chart)
Family                     Spread (N)
Within unit (4 jaws)          1.1
Between unit (cycles)         4.6
Temporal (hours)              0.9
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Within-unit variation dominates, so jaw-to-jaw X's come first","Temporal variation dominates, so hour-to-hour X's come first","No family dominates, so the study must be repeated","Between-unit variation dominates, so cycle-to-cycle X's come first"],answer:3,hint:"Pick the largest printed spread.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The largest family directs the next step."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Between unit = 4.6 N, far above 1.1 and 0.9, so cycle-to-cycle X's (set-up, material, timing) come first."}],final:"Between-unit (4.6 N) dominates → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 1.1 N is much smaller than 4.6 N.","Incorrect — temporal is the smallest family at 0.9 N.","Incorrect — the between-unit spread is clearly the largest.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-493",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A multi-vari chart suggested that handle time differs by shift. The team ran this test (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
One-way ANOVA: Handle_Time versus Shift
Source  DF      SS      MS      F      P
Shift    2   48.62   24.31  97.24  0.000
Error   42   10.50    0.25
Total   44   59.12
S = 0.5000   R-Sq = 82.24%
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Reject H₀: at least one shift mean differs, confirming the temporal family","Fail to reject H₀: the shift means are equal","Reject H₀: every shift mean differs from every other shift mean","The test proves that shift practices cause the longer handle times"],answer:0,hint:"Compare p with α, then word the decision carefully.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"p < α → reject H₀; ANOVA concludes that **at least one** mean differs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"p = 0.000 < 0.05, so the shift (temporal) family is statistically confirmed; its X's are investigated next."}],final:"p = 0.000 → reject H₀, temporal family confirmed → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — p = 0.000 is below 0.05, so H₀ is rejected.","Incorrect — ANOVA shows at least one mean differs, not that all differ.","Incorrect — the test confirms a difference between shifts, not its cause."],bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-494",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A multi-vari chart of **wall thickness (mm)** seemed to show a day-to-day difference. The team tested it (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
One-way ANOVA: Thickness versus Day
Source  DF       SS       MS     F      P
Day      2   0.0042   0.0021  0.60  0.555
Error   33   0.1155   0.0035
Total   35   0.1197
R-Sq = 3.51%
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Reject H₀: the temporal family is confirmed","Fail to reject H₀: there is not enough evidence that the days differ","Accept H₀: the day means are proven to be equal","The chart must be wrong, so the multi-vari study should be discarded"],answer:1,hint:"p above α — and watch the wording.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"p > α → fail to reject H₀ (never 'accept')."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"p = 0.555 > 0.05: the apparent day effect is not statistically supported; look at the other families."}],final:"p = 0.555 → fail to reject H₀ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — p = 0.555 is above 0.05, so H₀ is not rejected.","","Incorrect — a test never proves H₀; the correct wording is 'fail to reject'.","Incorrect — the test only says this family is not confirmed; other families remain to be examined."],trap:"'Accept H₀' is always the wrong wording.",bookRef:"Book p. 247"},{id:"sv-3-495",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Readings of **cap height** come from 4 mould cavities over 3 consecutive cycles (balanced, 36 readings). Two one-way ANOVAs were run on the same data (α = 0.05):

\`\`\`
One-way ANOVA: Height versus Cavity
Source  DF      SS      MS      F      P
Cavity   3  0.3360  0.1120  28.00  0.000
Error   32  0.1280  0.0040

One-way ANOVA: Height versus Cycle
Source  DF      SS      MS     F      P
Cycle    2  0.0060  0.0030  0.22  0.807
Error   33  0.4580  0.0139
\`\`\`

Which family of variation is statistically confirmed?`,options:["Between-unit (cycle-to-cycle)","Temporal","Within-unit (cavity-to-cavity)","Neither family, because one p-value is high"],answer:2,hint:"Cavities of one shot are positions inside one unit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The cavities of one cycle are made at the same moment → within-unit (positional) family."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Cavity p = 0.000 < 0.05 (confirmed); cycle p = 0.807 > 0.05 (not confirmed)."}],final:"Cavity p = 0.000 → within-unit confirmed → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the cycle test gives p = 0.807, so no cycle effect is shown.","Incorrect — no time-period factor was tested here.","","Incorrect — the cavity test has p = 0.000, which is significant."],bookRef:"Book pp. 243, 247"},{id:"sv-3-496",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Cell means behind a multi-vari chart of **API latency (ms)**, 3 routers polled in the same snapshot, 3 periods:

\`\`\`
Means by Period          Means by Router
09:00   18.1             A   22.9
14:00   21.3             B   23.8
20:00   29.9             C   22.5
(Router B is the highest in every snapshot)
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is CORRECT?`,options:["Positional variation dominates, because router B is always highest","Cyclical variation dominates, because the routers repeat in every snapshot","No family dominates, because every router shows the same time pattern","Temporal variation dominates, so time-related X's such as busy-hour load come first"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the spread of period means with the spread of router means.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Dominant family = largest spread, not most consistent pattern."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Period means move from 18.1 to 29.9 ms; router means stay between 22.5 and 23.8 ms. Router B is a real but small signal."}],final:"Temporal dominates; router B is minor → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — router means span only about 1 ms; consistency is not size.","Incorrect — routers in one snapshot are positions, and their spread is small.","Incorrect — period means differ by about 12 ms, a clearly dominant family.",""],trap:"Router B is consistent, but small.",bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-497",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Black Belt checks her multi-vari data before interpreting the chart:

\`\`\`
Coverage check
Spread captured in study          0.19 mm
Usual process spread (baseline)   0.25 mm
Captured / usual                  76%
Book guideline                    80%
\`\`\`

What should she do?`,options:["Extend the study to capture more of the usual variation","Interpret the chart, because 76% is close enough to 80%","Interpret the chart, because any coverage above 50% is acceptable","Stop, because the measurement system has failed"],answer:0,hint:"Is the printed coverage above or below the guideline?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The study should show at least 80% of the usual variation (step 4: is the variation exposed?)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"76% < 80% → collect more data (more periods or busier conditions) before interpreting."}],final:"76% < 80% → extend the study → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the guideline is a minimum; 76% is below it.","Incorrect — the book's guideline is 80%, not 50%.","Incorrect — coverage says nothing about the gauge; that is judged with MSA."],bookRef:"Book p. 242"},{id:"sv-3-498",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team entered its factors into Minitab in this order:

\`\`\`
Multi-Vari Chart for Fill_ml
Factor 1: Head    (positions within one cycle)
Factor 2: Cycle   (consecutive cycles within an hour)
Factor 3: Hour
\`\`\`

Which statement about this set-up is CORRECT?`,options:["It is wrong, because the temporal factor must always be entered first","It is the classical within, between, temporal order","It is wrong, because Minitab accepts only two factors","The order does not matter, because the chart always looks the same"],answer:1,hint:"Inside-out: positions, units, periods.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Classical order = within (head), between (cycle), temporal (hour)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The set-up follows it exactly; other orders may be tried if they show the variation more clearly."}],final:"Head → Cycle → Hour = classical order → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — temporal comes last in the classical order.","","Incorrect — Minitab accepts up to four factors.","Incorrect — the book says factor order changes how the chart looks."],bookRef:"Book p. 253"},{id:"sv-3-499",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Cell means of **handle time (min)** behind a two-factor multi-vari chart:

\`\`\`
Means of Handle_Time
              Day shift   Night shift
Queue A          6.1          6.2
Queue B          6.0          9.4
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST supported?`,options:["Queue B is slower on every shift","The night shift is slower for every queue","The effect of queue depends on the shift, suggesting an interaction","Neither factor matters, because three of the four means are similar"],answer:2,hint:"Does the queue effect stay the same across shifts?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book notes that a multi-vari chart can reveal **two-way interactions**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Queue B is slow only on the night shift: the queue effect depends on the shift — an interaction to confirm with a test."}],final:"Queue effect only on night shift → interaction → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — on the day shift queue B (6.0) is no slower than queue A (6.1).","Incorrect — queue A is about the same on both shifts (6.1 and 6.2).","","Incorrect — the 9.4 cell is far above the others and must be explained."],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-500",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A multi-vari chart of **picking time (min)** showed the within-wave (picker-to-picker) family to be by far the largest. Out of interest the team also tested the day factor (α = 0.05), using 300 readings:

\`\`\`
One-way ANOVA: Pick_Time versus Day
Source   DF      SS      MS     F      P
Day       4    6.48    1.62  3.16  0.014
Error   295  151.20    0.51
Total   299  157.68
R-Sq = 4.11%
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["Day is the dominant family, because its p-value is below 0.05","Day is not significant, because R-Sq is below 50%","The within-wave family must be wrong, because Day was significant","Day is statistically significant but explains little variation, so the picker family remains the focus"],answer:3,hint:"Separate 'is there an effect?' from 'how big is it?'.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"p answers whether an effect exists; R-Sq shows how much variation it explains."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"p = 0.014 < 0.05 but R-Sq = 4.11%: a real but small temporal effect. The large picker-to-picker family stays the priority."}],final:"Significant but small → keep the picker focus → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — significance is not size; Day explains only about 4% of the variation.","Incorrect — significance is judged by p, not R-Sq; p = 0.014 is significant.","Incorrect — both can be true; a small significant effect does not cancel a large family.",""],trap:"Statistical significance is not the same as being the dominant family.",bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-501",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In the Analyze phase, a multi-vari study requires the team to change process settings deliberately. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Is the Analyze multi-vari study active or passive?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book calls it a **passive** study at this point in DMAIC."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"No settings are changed, so the statement is false."}],final:"Passive study → False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that describes a designed experiment; the multi-vari study observes the process as it runs.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 241"},{id:"sv-3-502",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:2,stem:"In a multi-vari study, variation between the morning shift and the night shift is an example of cyclical variation. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Shifts repeat, but which family covers time periods?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Temporal variation = shift-to-shift, day-to-day, week-to-week."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Morning versus night shift is temporal, so the statement is false."}],final:"Shift-to-shift = temporal → False → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — shift-to-shift differences are temporal; cyclical compares consecutive units or batches.",""],trap:"'Cyclical' sounds like repeating shifts, but it means consecutive units.",bookRef:"Book p. 243"},{id:"sv-3-503",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`In what order should a multi-vari chart be read?

1. Compare the three family sizes and name the dominant one.
2. Look at the movement of the unit means within each period.
3. Look at the length of each unit's vertical line (spread inside the unit).
4. Look at the gaps between the period means.`,options:["3, 2, 4, 1","1, 3, 2, 4","4, 2, 3, 1","3, 4, 2, 1"],answer:0,hint:"Read inside-out, then compare.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Within (line length) → between (unit-mean jumps) → temporal (period-mean gaps) → compare sizes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is 3, 2, 4, 1."}],final:"Within → between → temporal → compare → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the families must be sized before they can be compared.","Incorrect — this reads outside-in; the chart is read from the innermost family outward.","Incorrect — between-unit movement is read before the temporal gaps."],bookRef:"Book pp. 246–247"},{id:"sv-3-504",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Put these stages of narrowing the X's into the order the book describes:

1. Confirm the vital few X's with hypothesis tests.
2. Brainstorm possible X's on a fishbone and process map.
3. Run a multi-vari study to find the dominant family.
4. Rate probable X's with the X-Y Matrix and FMEA.`,options:["2, 3, 4, 1","2, 4, 3, 1","4, 2, 3, 1","2, 4, 1, 3"],answer:1,hint:"Possible → probable → sifted → proven.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The X funnel: possible X's (fishbone, map) → probable X's (X-Y Matrix, FMEA) → X-sifting (multi-vari) → vital few confirmed with tests."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is 2, 4, 3, 1."}],final:"Fishbone → X-Y/FMEA → multi-vari → tests → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the X-Y Matrix and FMEA (Measure) come before multi-vari (Analyze).","","Incorrect — possible X's must be brainstormed before they can be rated.","Incorrect — multi-vari screens first; hypothesis tests confirm afterwards."],bookRef:"Book p. 240"},{id:"sv-3-505",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`A Black Belt designs a multi-vari sampling plan for **fibre splice loss**. In which order should she make these decisions?

1. Choose how many time periods (days) to cover.
2. Define what one unit is (e.g. one splice tray).
3. Choose how many consecutive units to sample in each period.
4. Choose the positions to measure within each unit.`,options:["1, 3, 4, 2","4, 2, 3, 1","2, 4, 3, 1","2, 1, 4, 3"],answer:2,hint:"Define the unit first and the families follow.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The plan is nested: positions within units, units within periods, so the unit is defined first."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Unit (2) → positions in it (4) → consecutive units per period (3) → periods (1)."}],final:"Unit → positions → units per period → periods → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the unit must be defined before its positions or the number of units can be chosen.","Incorrect — positions are 'within a unit', so the unit is defined first.","","Incorrect — this breaks the inside-out nesting the plan follows."],bookRef:"Book pp. 242–244"},{id:"sv-3-506",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Before and after drawing a multi-vari chart, the team works with its list of potential X's. Put these steps in order:

1. Focus further work on the X's of the largest family.
2. Assign each X to the family it would create, splitting with percentages if needed.
3. List the potential X's from the Measure-phase X-Y Matrix.
4. Draw the chart and find the largest family.`,options:["3, 4, 2, 1","2, 3, 4, 1","3, 2, 1, 4","3, 2, 4, 1"],answer:3,hint:"List, assign, chart, focus.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: list X's from the X-Y Matrix, assign each to a family, graph, then focus on the largest family's X's."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is 3, 2, 4, 1."}],final:"X-Y list → assign → chart → focus → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book assigns X's to families before graphing.","Incorrect — the X's must be listed before they can be assigned.","Incorrect — the focus is chosen after the chart shows the largest family.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 245"},{id:"sv-3-507",phase:3,topic:"3.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put the events of the book's **bottle-fill** exercise in order:

1. The multi-vari chart showed within-unit variation was largest.
2. A capability study showed the mean fill far above the upper limit (negative Cpk).
3. The in-line scale was found to be disturbed by air flow.
4. Variation was reduced, then the mean was moved toward target.`,options:["2, 1, 3, 4","1, 2, 3, 4","2, 3, 1, 4","2, 1, 4, 3"],answer:0,hint:"Baseline, picture, cause, fix.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book sequence: wide, off-target fill (Cpk negative) → multi-vari shows within-unit largest → scale disturbed by air flow → reduce variation, then shift the mean."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"That is 2, 1, 3, 4."}],final:"Capability → chart → scale → variation then mean → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the baseline capability came before the multi-vari study.","Incorrect — the chart pointed the team to the scale, so the chart came first.","Incorrect — the cause had to be found before variation could be reduced."],bookRef:"Book p. 253"},{id:"sv-3-508",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which two parameters fully define a **binomial** distribution?",options:["The mean and the standard deviation","The number of trials n and the probability p of the counted outcome","The average rate λ of events per interval","The shape β and the scale η"],answer:1,hint:"The binomial counts outcomes in a fixed number of yes/no trials.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Binomial: fixed number of independent trials **n**, each with the same probability **p** of the counted outcome."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"μ and σ → normal; λ → Poisson; shape and scale → Weibull."}],final:"Binomial = n and p → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — mean and standard deviation define the normal distribution.","","Incorrect — a single rate λ defines the Poisson distribution.","Incorrect — shape and scale parameters define a Weibull distribution."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-509",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which property is the signature of a **Poisson** distribution?",options:["Its mean equals its median and mode","It has a fixed maximum equal to the number of trials","Its mean equals its variance","It is memoryless"],answer:2,hint:"One parameter, λ, does two jobs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a Poisson count, the single parameter λ is both the **mean and the variance**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"If counts have a variance far above the mean, the simple Poisson model does not fit."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Mean = median = mode is normal; fixed maximum is binomial; memoryless is exponential."}],final:"Poisson: mean = variance → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — that is the signature of the symmetric normal distribution.","Incorrect — a fixed maximum n belongs to the binomial; Poisson counts have no upper limit.","","Incorrect — memorylessness belongs to the exponential distribution of times between events."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-510",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which pair of control charts is based on the **binomial** distribution?",options:["c and u charts","I-MR and Xbar-R charts","EWMA and CuSum charts","p and np charts"],answer:3,hint:"Defective units out of a known sample size.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Binomial counts **defective units** out of n; p and np charts track defectives."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"c and u (defects) → Poisson; I-MR, Xbar-R, EWMA, CuSum → continuous data."}],final:"Binomial → p and np charts → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — c and u charts count defects and rest on the Poisson distribution.","Incorrect — these variables charts are built for continuous, roughly normal data.","Incorrect — these are time-weighted charts for small shifts in continuous data.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-511",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Router failures occur at random at a constant rate, and the time between failures is modelled with an **exponential** distribution. What does this model assume?",options:["The chance of a failure in the next hour does not depend on how long the router has run","Failures become more likely as the router ages","The time between failures is symmetric around its mean","Failures always arrive at exactly equal intervals"],answer:0,hint:"Think of 'memoryless' random failures.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The exponential models times between independent random events at a **constant rate**; it is memoryless."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Past running time does not change the chance of the next failure — it suits random failures, not wear-out."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Wear-out, symmetry and fixed intervals contradict the model."}],final:"Exponential = constant rate, memoryless → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an ageing (wear-out) pattern needs a model such as Weibull with an increasing failure rate.","Incorrect — the exponential is strongly right-skewed.","Incorrect — equal intervals would be deterministic, not random."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-512",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What are the **four classes** of non-normal data described in the book?",options:["Skewness, outliers, autocorrelation and bias","Skewness, kurtosis, multiple modes and granularity","Binomial, Poisson, exponential and uniform","Positional, cyclical, temporal and random"],answer:1,hint:"The book classifies non-normal shapes.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book classes: **skewness, kurtosis, multiple modes, granularity**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Probability models and multi-vari families are different lists."}],final:"Four classes: skew, kurtosis, modes, granularity → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — autocorrelation and bias are not among the book's four classes.","","Incorrect — these are probability models, not the book's classes of non-normal data.","Incorrect — the first three are multi-vari families of variation."],bookRef:"Book p. 255"},{id:"sv-3-513",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which class of non-normal data does the book describe as a **natural state** for data that run near a limit?",options:["Granularity","Kurtosis","Multiple modes","Skewness"],answer:3,hint:"Time cannot be below zero.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: skewness is a natural state for data near a natural or artificial limit (e.g. time ≥ 0)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The other three classes are usually symptoms of a problem to separate and correct."}],final:"Skewness can be natural → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — granularity usually signals poor measurement resolution.","Incorrect — kurtosis usually signals mixtures, sorting or trends.","Incorrect — multiple modes usually signal mixed sources.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 257, 264"},{id:"sv-3-514",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, what is the MOST likely cause of **multiple modes** in a data set?",options:["Poor measurement resolution","A natural limit such as zero","A mixture of distributions from different sources","A large sample size"],answer:2,hint:"Two machines, two operators, two peaks.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: multiple modes come mostly from **mixtures** (3-1), also lack of independence and catastrophic failures."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A bimodal set is often two normal sets combined."}],final:"Mixtures → multiple modes → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — poor resolution causes granularity.","Incorrect — a natural limit typically causes skewness.","","Incorrect — sample size does not create separate peaks."],bookRef:"Book p. 261"},{id:"sv-3-515",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"A histogram shows one extreme outlier. What does the book say about a **single** outlier compared with **multiple** outliers?",options:["A single outlier should always be deleted; multiple outliers should be kept","Both mean the process is normal but the sample is too small","A single outlier means granularity; multiple outliers mean skewness","A single outlier usually has its own cause; multiple outliers usually involve several inputs"],answer:3,hint:"One outlier is easy to isolate.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: one extreme outlier usually has **its own cause**, found by where it sits on the X axis; multiple outliers usually mean **several inputs** are involved."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Deleting, blaming sample size or relabelling the class are not the book's advice."}],final:"One outlier = own cause; many = several inputs → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book does not say to delete outliers; it says to find their cause.","Incorrect — outliers are clues to causes, not a sample-size issue.","Incorrect — outliers are not the same as granularity or skewness.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 262"},{id:"sv-3-516",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the binomial and Poisson distributions is CORRECT?",options:["The binomial counts defective units out of n; the Poisson counts defects or events in an interval","The binomial counts defects with no fixed maximum per unit","The Poisson counts defective units out of a known sample size","Both are continuous distributions drawn as smooth curves"],answer:0,hint:"Defective unit versus defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Binomial = defective units out of a known n; Poisson = defects or events per interval, no fixed maximum."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Two options swap the definitions; both distributions are discrete."}],final:"Binomial = defectives; Poisson = defects → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — that describes the Poisson distribution.","Incorrect — that describes the binomial distribution.","Incorrect — both are discrete and are drawn as bars."],trap:"Defect versus defective decides binomial versus Poisson.",bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-517",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about **skewness** is CORRECT?",options:["A right-skewed data set has its mean below its median","The longest tail points in the direction of the skew","A skewness coefficient near +2 indicates symmetric data","Skewness is always a sign of a problem that must be removed"],answer:1,hint:"Name the skew after the tail.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book: the **longest tail points in the direction of the skew**; coefficient ≈ 0 symmetric, > 0 right, < 0 left."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"In right skew the mean is pulled toward the tail (book example: mean 70.0, median 65.7)."}],final:"Tail direction = skew direction → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — in right skew the long tail pulls the mean above the median.","","Incorrect — about 0 indicates symmetry; +2 is strong right skew.","Incorrect — the book says skewness is often a natural state."],bookRef:"Book p. 257"},{id:"sv-3-518",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the causes of non-normal data are CORRECT?

1. Granularity is usually caused by a mixture of two machines.
2. A natural limit, such as time not going below zero, can cause skewness.
3. An interaction between two inputs can cause skewness.
4. A platykurtic shape is caused by stacking distributions with very different variances.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Check each cause against the book's lists for each class.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Skewness causes include natural limits and interactions; granularity comes from resolution or categories; very different variances give a peaked (leptokurtic) shape."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Statements 2 and 3 are true; 1 and 4 attach the wrong cause."}],final:"True pair = 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statement 1 is false: granularity comes from measurement resolution or step-type data.","","Incorrect — statement 4 is false: very different variances give a leptokurtic (peaked) shape.","Incorrect — both are false: granularity is resolution, and very different variances give a peaked shape."],bookRef:"Book pp. 257–263"},{id:"sv-3-519",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

Granular data, with only a few distinct values, are most often caused by poor measurement-system [?].`,options:["accuracy","linearity","resolution","stability"],answer:2,hint:"The gauge cannot see small differences.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book cause 4-1 of granularity: **measurement system resolution**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Check the Gage R&R number of distinct categories, or record more decimal places."}],final:"Resolution → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — accuracy (bias) shifts values but does not create steps.","Incorrect — linearity is bias changing across the range, not few distinct values.","","Incorrect — stability is bias changing over time."],bookRef:"Book p. 263"},{id:"sv-3-520",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

When defects occur independently at a constant average rate (a Poisson process), the time between consecutive defects follows the [?] distribution.`,options:["binomial","exponential","normal","uniform"],answer:1,hint:"Counts per interval are Poisson; what describes the gaps?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poisson counts events per interval; the **exponential** describes the time between those events."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Binomial counts trials; normal is symmetric; uniform is flat."}],final:"Gaps of a Poisson process → exponential → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the binomial counts outcomes in a fixed number of trials.","","Incorrect — times between random events are right-skewed, not symmetric.","Incorrect — a uniform distribution makes every value in a range equally likely."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-521",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"The book lists causes of **multiple modes**. All of the following are on that list EXCEPT:",options:["Mixtures of distributions","Lack of independence such as trends or patterns","Measurement system resolution","Catastrophic failures"],answer:2,hint:"One option belongs to granularity.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Multiple modes: mixtures, lack of independence, catastrophic failures."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"**Measurement resolution** causes granularity, not separate peaks."}],final:"Resolution → granularity, not modes → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — mixtures are the most likely cause (3-1).","Incorrect — this is cause 3-2.","","Incorrect — this is cause 3-3, e.g. a shorted motor reading zero."],bookRef:"Book pp. 261, 263"},{id:"sv-3-522",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which count would NOT normally be modelled with a **Poisson** distribution?",options:["Scratches found on each painted panel","Customer calls arriving per minute","Typing errors per page of a contract","Defective SIM cards in a sample of exactly 50"],answer:3,hint:"Which count has a fixed number of yes/no trials?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Poisson = defects or events per interval with no fixed maximum."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Defective cards out of exactly 50 = fixed n, pass/fail → **binomial**."}],final:"Defectives out of fixed n → binomial → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — defects per unit with no fixed maximum fit the Poisson.","Incorrect — independent arrivals per interval fit the Poisson.","Incorrect — errors per page with no fixed maximum fit the Poisson.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"},{id:"sv-3-523",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"End-of-line **motor current** tests mostly read between 11.5 A and 12.5 A in a normal-looking group, but a separate cluster of readings sits at 0 A. Which cause of multiple modes does this MOST likely show?",options:["A catastrophic failure","A mixture of two machines","Poor gauge resolution","A natural limit at zero"],answer:0,hint:"What makes a test read exactly zero?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book cause 3-3 of multiple modes: **catastrophic failures**, e.g. a motor shorts out and the test reads zero."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A separate cluster at 0 A beside a normal group fits that pattern."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Mixtures, resolution and natural limits give other shapes."}],final:"Cluster at zero = catastrophic failure → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a second machine would give another hump near a similar value, not readings of zero.","Incorrect — poor resolution gives steps across the range, not a cluster at zero.","Incorrect — a natural limit makes data pile up toward the limit; here two separate groups exist."],bookRef:"Book p. 261"},{id:"sv-3-524",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A histogram of incoming **shaft diameters** from a supplier is cut off sharply at both specification limits, with almost no values outside them. Which cause BEST explains this shape?",options:["A mixture of two production lines","Sorting or screening by the supplier","Granular measurement resolution","A catastrophic failure in the process"],answer:1,hint:"Who removed the values outside the limits?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book lists **sorting or selecting** (scrapping product outside the limits) as a cause of non-normal shapes."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Sharp edges exactly at the specification limits mean the out-of-spec parts were screened out."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Mixtures, granularity and failures give other patterns."}],final:"Cut-offs at the spec limits = sorting → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a mixture usually gives two humps, not sharp cut-offs at the limits.","","Incorrect — granularity gives steps of a few values, not cut-offs at the limits.","Incorrect — a catastrophic failure gives a separate cluster of extreme values."],bookRef:"Book pp. 257, 260"},{id:"sv-3-525",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Dropped-call rates are low on most days but rise sharply only on days with **both** very high traffic **and** a particular software release. Either condition alone has little effect. Which cause of skewed data does this illustrate?",options:["Interaction","Natural limits","Non-linear relationship","Sorting"],answer:0,hint:"Two inputs together have a bigger effect than each alone.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book cause 5 of skewness: **interactions** — two inputs together have a bigger effect than either alone (the aerosol-plus-flame example)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"High traffic and the release only matter together, producing a long right tail."}],final:"Combined effect = interaction → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a natural limit is a boundary such as zero, not two inputs acting together.","Incorrect — non-linearity is one X affecting Y in a curved way, not two inputs combining.","Incorrect — sorting removes values outside limits."],bookRef:"Book p. 258"},{id:"sv-3-526",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"Received **signal strength** at a cell site is roughly normal, but the **throughput** it produces responds to signal strength along a steep curve. A histogram of throughput is clearly skewed. Which cause BEST explains the skew?",options:["A mixture of two cell sites","Measurement resolution","A non-linear relationship between X and Y","A catastrophic failure"],answer:2,hint:"A normal X can give a non-normal Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book cause 4 of skewness: **non-linear relationships** — a normal X passed through a curved response gives a non-normal Y."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Normal signal strength + steep curved response → skewed throughput."}],final:"Normal X through a curve → skewed Y → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — only one site is involved; the skew comes from how Y responds to X.","Incorrect — resolution creates granular steps, not a smooth skew.","","Incorrect — a catastrophic failure creates a separate cluster of extreme values."],bookRef:"Book p. 258"},{id:"sv-3-527",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing team's **invoice processing time** is right-skewed. Further digging shows the data mix electronic invoices (fast) with paper invoices (much slower) that were expected to behave the same. Which cause of skewness is this?",options:["Artificial limits","Mixtures","Natural limits","Trends over time"],answer:1,hint:"Two sources that should be the same but are not.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Book cause 3 of skewness: **mixtures** — data from sources that should be the same but are not (e.g. payment method)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Separate electronic and paper invoices and analyse each stream."}],final:"Two invoice types mixed = mixture → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — artificial limits come from reporting or screening rules, not two invoice types.","","Incorrect — time cannot go below zero, but the stem identifies two different sources.","Incorrect — nothing in the stem changes over time."],bookRef:"Book p. 257"},{id:"sv-3-528",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Graphical summary for **time to restore service (h)**:

\`\`\`
Summary Report for Restore_h
N          150
Minimum    0.20
1st Q      0.80
Median     1.40
Mean       2.60
3rd Q      2.90
Maximum   19.50
Skewness   3.10
Anderson-Darling  P-Value < 0.005
\`\`\`

Which interpretation is BEST?`,options:["The data are left-skewed, so the median is pulled below the mean","The data are normal, because the mean and median are both close to 2","The data are granular, so the gauge resolution must be fixed first","The data are right-skewed against the natural limit of zero, which can be a natural state"],answer:3,hint:"Read the sign of skewness and the position of the mean.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Skewness +3.10 and a maximum of 19.5 h → long right tail; the mean (2.60) is pulled above the median (1.40)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Restore time cannot go below zero, a natural limit; the book calls skewness a natural state here, so use median-based thinking rather than 'fixing' the shape."}],final:"Right skew near a natural limit → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — positive skewness and a long upper tail mean right skew.","Incorrect — AD p < 0.005 rejects normality, and the mean is well above the median.","Incorrect — nothing shows a few repeated values; the shape is a long tail.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 257, 264"},{id:"sv-3-529",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`**Gap width (mm)** from two machines feeding one line was tested for normality separately and combined:

\`\`\`
Anderson-Darling normality tests
Data set           N    Mean    StDev   AD P-Value
Machine A         60   2.012   0.011     0.412
Machine B         60   2.058   0.012     0.563
Combined (A+B)   120   2.035   0.026   < 0.005
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["The combined data are non-normal mainly because two normal streams are mixed","Both machines are non-normal, so the line needs a transformation","The combined data are normal, because each machine is normal","Machine B is the cause because its p-value is the highest"],answer:0,hint:"Each stream is fine on its own; what changes when they are combined?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A mixture of two normal sources with different means is a classic cause of non-normality (multiple modes)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A and B each fail to reject normality (p 0.412 and 0.563), but combined p < 0.005: the means differ (2.012 vs 2.058), so analyse the machines separately."}],final:"Two normal streams mixed → non-normal → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — each machine has p > 0.05, so neither fails the normality test.","Incorrect — p < 0.005 rejects normality for the combined set.","Incorrect — a higher normality p-value does not identify a cause."],bookRef:"Book pp. 257, 261"},{id:"sv-3-530",phase:3,topic:"3.1.2",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`Weekly counts of **major incidents** on a platform over one year:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: Incidents_per_week
N     Mean    Variance   Minimum   Maximum
52    4.0      12.0        0         17
\`\`\`

A simple Poisson model is proposed. Which conclusion is BEST?`,options:["The Poisson model fits well, because the mean is a whole number","The variance is far above the mean, suggesting clustered incidents that a simple Poisson model would understate","The data should be modelled as binomial, because incidents are counted","The data are normal, because the maximum is less than five times the mean"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the Poisson's signature property.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"For a Poisson count, mean = variance."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Variance 12.0 is three times the mean 4.0: incidents cluster (one change triggering several), so a simple Poisson model is too optimistic — itself a clue for Analyze."}],final:"Variance ≫ mean → not simple Poisson → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a whole-number mean says nothing about fit.","","Incorrect — there is no fixed number of trials per week, so binomial does not apply.","Incorrect — there is no such normality rule; counts this skewed are not normal."],bookRef:"Syllabus 3.1.2"}];export{e as items};
